And you squash them into one single clean projection before you merge it into the main project.

37:20.920 --> 37:24.420
You are intentionally destroying your own procedural embarrassment.

37:24.560 --> 37:25.840
Yes, you're curating the history.

37:25.840 --> 37:29.900
And then there's force pushing, which is the ultimate Git override.

37:30.180 --> 37:35.000
It literally replaces the authoritative history of the branch with a completely new reality.

37:35.420 --> 37:40.140
If you force push, you are telling the system to forget the old timeline completely.

37:40.560 --> 37:45.860
And don't forget garbage collection, which is an automated process that permanently deletes unreferenced,

37:46.060 --> 37:49.460
abandoned material for the repository just to save space.

37:49.860 --> 37:50.300
Wow.

37:50.520 --> 37:53.180
Those operations, while they vary in their technical mechanics,

37:53.180 --> 37:58.020
all share the core feature of destroying distinctions among prior histories.

37:58.960 --> 38:05.720
Returning to a deleted branch in Git is only possible because the material was never truly foreclosed from the total space.

38:05.860 --> 38:09.460
It merely lost its privileged, authoritative position.

38:09.460 --> 38:11.400
So it was just hidden, not gone.

38:11.580 --> 38:11.800
Right.

38:12.440 --> 38:16.580
True foreclosure, the irreversible destruction we talked about earlier with the one-word constraint,

38:16.800 --> 38:22.660
only happens when the reconstructive information itself is annihilated, like in a garbage collection sweep.

38:23.060 --> 38:26.420
So even when we build a machine explicitly designed to remember everything,

38:26.800 --> 38:29.900
we still inevitably build tools inside it to help us forget.

38:30.160 --> 38:31.400
We want to squash our commits.

38:31.520 --> 38:33.320
