Science would grind to an absolute halt.

40:11.820 --> 40:12.460
It would freeze.

40:12.600 --> 40:14.400
Human progress would just freeze.

40:14.400 --> 40:20.060
We trade reconstructive fidelity knowing exactly how the sausage was made for portability.

40:20.060 --> 40:27.420
The final structure, the state description, is infinitely cheaper to circulate, transmit, and reuse than the history of its creation.

40:27.420 --> 40:30.700
That is the exact mathematical trade-off Flickshinn isolates.

40:31.020 --> 40:36.100
Histories generate structures, but structures are vastly easier to store and transmit.

40:36.740 --> 40:42.760
A reader who wants to apply a theorem doesn't need to know which of the author's earlier formulations were wrong and why.

40:43.020 --> 40:46.100
They just need the region of truth that publication retains.

40:46.100 --> 40:48.120
They just need the final math.

40:48.440 --> 40:48.820
Exactly.

40:49.500 --> 41:03.180
The equivalence relation of publication identifying every history that arrives at the same final text, whether it took a day or a decade, is precisely the mechanism that converts a historical construction into a usable state description.

41:03.180 --> 41:09.620
As Flickshinn states so perfectly, a ladder that cannot be discarded isn't much of a ladder at all.

41:10.120 --> 41:11.320
It would be a permanent burden.

41:11.840 --> 41:13.960
We erase the process so the product can travel.

